# AGENT.md — src/

Application root for the Onion Architecture.

## What lives here

- `main.ts` — starts the server{{#if hasOrm}} and initializes the ORM connection{{/if}}. Nothing else.
- `server.ts` — builds the Hapi server (initServer factory), registers plugins
  and routes, wires every module's dependency chain manually.
- `config/` — environment loading (`env.ts` is the only file that reads `process.env`).
- `plugins/` — Hapi plugin registrations (swagger{{#if useAuth}}, auth{{/if}}).
- `errors/` — AppError base class and subclasses (NotFoundError, ValidationError).
- `shared/` — cross-cutting infrastructure (database, cache{{#if useAuth}}, auth{{/if}}{{#if useHealthCheck}}, health{{/if}}).

## Onion Architecture layers

```
src/
├── domain/          ← Innermost: entities, repository interfaces (ports)
├── application/     ← Use cases, DTOs, service implementations
├── infrastructure/  ← Persistence adapters (TypeORM, Prisma, Mongoose, in-memory)
└── presentation/    ← Outermost: Hapi route handlers and route definitions
```

Dependency direction is strictly inward — `presentation → application → domain`.
Infrastructure implements domain interfaces; domain knows nothing about persistence.

## Rules

- Do not put business logic in `main.ts` or `server.ts`.
- When you add a new module, register its routes in `server.ts` with
  `server.route(moduleRoutes)` in the same change.
- Plugins (swagger, auth) are registered in `server.ts` via
  `await server.register([...])`.
- Cross-cutting concerns (error formatting) use `server.ext('onPreResponse', ...)`.
{{#if useAuth}}
- Auth is set up as a JWT strategy in `plugins/auth.ts`. Apply it to a
  route by setting `options: { auth: 'jwt' }` in the route config.
{{/if}}
